1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,305
Laddoo Lab has ads, offers and ten shops.

2
00:00:03,305 --> 00:00:06,012
But few people know the story behind it.

3
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:09,133
Then a local reporter
visits Nani's kitchen.

4
00:00:09,683 --> 00:00:12,320
The next morning, Nani
is in the newspaper.

5
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:14,089
And that week, orders jump.

6
00:00:14,739 --> 00:00:16,881
Zoya did not pay for that story.

7
00:00:16,881 --> 00:00:17,948
She earned it.

8
00:00:18,298 --> 00:00:22,998
The reporter came because the story was
real: a grandmother's millet recipe,

9
00:00:22,998 --> 00:00:24,074
made by hand.

10
00:00:24,424 --> 00:00:27,734
This is the power of
public relations, or P R.

11
00:00:28,384 --> 00:00:30,308
So what is public relations?

12
00:00:30,608 --> 00:00:34,757
Kotler and Armstrong call it building
good relations with the company's

13
00:00:34,757 --> 00:00:39,252
publics, by earning good publicity,
building a good image, and handling

14
00:00:39,252 --> 00:00:40,917
bad rumours and stories.

15
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:45,479
A public is any group that cares about
your brand: customers, neighbours,

16
00:00:45,479 --> 00:00:48,165
the press, shopkeepers,
even the government.

17
00:00:48,815 --> 00:00:51,502
Let's see how the Nani
story really happened.

18
00:00:51,852 --> 00:00:52,752
Step one.

19
00:00:52,748 --> 00:00:54,267
Find a true story.

20
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:58,191
Dev notices that people love
hearing about Nani's recipe.

21
00:00:58,541 --> 00:00:59,441
Step two.

22
00:00:59,399 --> 00:01:00,582
Write it down.

23
00:01:00,582 --> 00:01:05,260
Dev writes a short press release,
a one-page news note for reporters.

24
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:06,629
Step three.

25
00:01:06,629 --> 00:01:08,046
Invite the press.

26
00:01:08,046 --> 00:01:12,209
Zoya asks a local reporter to
a tasting in Nani's kitchen.

27
00:01:12,559 --> 00:01:13,467
Step four.

28
00:01:13,467 --> 00:01:14,698
The story runs.

29
00:01:14,698 --> 00:01:18,882
A photo of Nani, and a headline
about healthy tiffin sweets.

30
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:20,132
Step five.

31
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:20,900
Share it.

32
00:01:20,723 --> 00:01:24,544
Dev posts the story, and every
shop sticks it up by the counter.

33
00:01:25,194 --> 00:01:26,432
Ads are paid for.

34
00:01:26,432 --> 00:01:27,993
Good stories are earned.

35
00:01:28,693 --> 00:01:31,708
A story in the news is just one P R tool.

36
00:01:31,708 --> 00:01:33,226
Here are three more.

37
00:01:33,576 --> 00:01:34,476
Events.

38
00:01:34,257 --> 00:01:37,716
Laddoo Lab runs a millet
tasting at a school fair.

39
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:39,226
Community work.

40
00:01:39,226 --> 00:01:42,622
Nani teaches a free
healthy-snack class for parents.

41
00:01:42,972 --> 00:01:44,263
And social media.

42
00:01:44,263 --> 00:01:48,188
Dev answers every question from
parents, kindly and quickly.

43
00:01:48,838 --> 00:01:50,805
Where do we see this at work today?

44
00:01:51,155 --> 00:01:52,055
News.

45
00:01:51,732 --> 00:01:55,659
A hospital shares its new heart
unit with the local paper.

46
00:01:56,009 --> 00:01:56,909
Events.

47
00:01:56,667 --> 00:02:00,103
A bank runs a free money
class for college students.

48
00:02:00,453 --> 00:02:01,570
Community work.

49
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:05,267
A bakery gives its extra bread
to a shelter every night.

50
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:07,036
Handling bad news.

51
00:02:07,036 --> 00:02:10,429
An airline explains a
delay quickly and honestly.

52
00:02:11,079 --> 00:02:13,734
But is P R always in your control?

53
00:02:13,734 --> 00:02:14,634
No.

54
00:02:14,586 --> 00:02:15,615
The good side.

55
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:19,512
People trust a news story more
than an ad, and it costs little.

56
00:02:19,862 --> 00:02:21,020
The hard side.

57
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:23,592
You can't control what reporters write.

58
00:02:23,592 --> 00:02:25,487
And bad news spreads fast.

59
00:02:25,837 --> 00:02:28,880
One day, a parent posts
that a pack was stale.

60
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:32,821
Zoya replies that same day, says
sorry, and replaces the pack.

61
00:02:33,171 --> 00:02:35,905
Telling the truth fast is an old idea.

62
00:02:35,905 --> 00:02:40,946
In 1906, after a train crash in America,
Ivy Lee told the railway to share

63
00:02:40,946 --> 00:02:42,754
the facts with reporters.

64
00:02:42,754 --> 00:02:46,382
His statement is often called
the first press release.

65
00:02:46,732 --> 00:02:47,632
The lesson?

66
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:51,265
Earn trust with the truth, most
of all when things go wrong.

67
00:02:51,915 --> 00:02:52,860
Quick check.

68
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:57,107
A reporter writes about Nani's
kitchen, and Laddoo Lab pays nothing.

69
00:02:57,107 --> 00:02:58,510
Which tool is this?

70
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:01,544
Public relations!

71
00:03:01,544 --> 00:03:03,736
The story was earned, not bought.

72
00:03:04,336 --> 00:03:05,349
So, remember.

73
00:03:05,649 --> 00:03:09,896
Public relations builds good relations
with your publics, and earns good

74
00:03:09,896 --> 00:03:10,796
stories.

75
00:03:10,930 --> 00:03:15,282
Its tools include news, events,
community work and social media.

76
00:03:15,632 --> 00:03:18,370
And when things go wrong,
tell the truth, fast.

77
00:03:18,970 --> 00:03:21,727
Next time: marketing planning and control.
